before:
new Promise(executor) calls its executor immediately with resolve (and an optional reject parameter). resolve(events) fulfills this promise with the recorded array. timerLabels returns that promise, and only its retained callback resolves it.
```javascript
function timerLabels(label) {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    const events = [];
    const discarded = setTimeout(() => { events.push("discarded"); }, 0);
    clearTimeout(discarded);
    setTimeout(() => {
      events.push(label);
      resolve(events);
    }, 0);
  });
}
console.log((await timerLabels("kept")).join(","));
```
Output: kept. No elapsed duration is asserted.

after:
This example keeps one timer and cancels another.
```javascript
function timerLabels(label) {
  return new Promise(resolve => {
    const events = [];
    const discarded = setTimeout(() => { events.push("discarded"); }, 0);
    clearTimeout(discarded);
    setTimeout(() => {
      events.push(label);
      resolve(events);
    }, 0);
  });
}
console.log((await timerLabels("kept")).join(","));
```
Output:
```text
kept
```
`new Promise` runs its setup function immediately. The second timer later adds the label and calls `resolve(events)` to supply the result. `await` waits for that result.
